why 3 5/8 SPACING The melody sits on its high G, the peak of the phrase, while the alto passes down G–F♯. The gap lasts one eighth and closes as the soprano falls to B.
why 4 0 LEAPS The hymn tune itself drops a sixth from its high G and keeps falling A–G to the cadence. Bach harmonizes the melody as it is; he did not write it.
why 5 1/4 DIRECT The tune leaps down D–A while the bass steps down to D. Hidden fifths with a step in the bass are mild, and the leap belongs to the hymn tune.
why 7 1/4 DIRECT The same figure as in bar 5: D–A in the tune over a bass stepping down to D, harmonized the same way.
why 10 0 LEAPS The same melodic cadence as bar 4: the hymn tune drops from its high G and keeps falling by step.
